Not every community benefited equally. In the segregated South, many Carnegie libraries were closed to Black residents, and only a small number of separate branches were built to serve them. The program's legacy, then, reflects both the promise of free public learning and the limits on who was allowed to share in it.$q$, $q$According to the passage, a town that accepted a Carnegie grant had to:$q$, $q$["charge residents a membership fee", "buy books that Carnegie chose", "name the library after Carnegie", "provide the land and fund the library each year"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Paragraph 2: towns had to "provide the land and promise to spend a set amount each year."$q$),
