Scientist 2: In most of these trees, red pigments (anthocyanins) are not present in summer leaves. As chlorophyll breaks down in the fall, the tree makes red pigments new. These pigments may shield the leaf from strong light while the tree pulls nutrients out of the leaf before it drops.$q$, $q$Which finding would most strongly support Scientist 2?$q$, $q$["Green leaves contain chlorophyll.", "Tests find almost no red pigment in maple leaves in July but large amounts in October.", "Some trees lose their leaves in winter.", "Tests find the same amount of red pigment in July and October."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Scientist 2 says red pigment is made new in fall, so little in July and a lot in October supports that. Equal amounts would support Scientist 1.$q$),
  ('ACT', $q$ACT Science$q$, $q$Applying a viewpoint$q$, 'mc', $q$Two scientists explain why some tree leaves turn red in autumn.

